/* book Worked example: Q: An insulated rigid tank of 0.2 m³ is evacuated. It is connected to a line carrying air at 500 kPa and 27 °C, and the valve is opened until the tank pressure reaches 500 kPa. Find the final temperature and the mass of air in the tank. Take R = 0.287 kJ/kg·K, cv = 0.718 kJ/kg·K, cp = 1.005 kJ/kg·K, γ = 1.4.
   1. Data: V = 0.2 m³, m₁ = 0, Ti = 27 + 273 = 300 K, p₂ = 500 kPa; Q = 0, W = 0, me = 0.
   2. Mass balance: m₂ = mi. Energy balance: mihi = m₂u₂, so u₂ = hi.
   3. Line gas: hi = cpTi = 1.005 × 300 = 301.5 kJ/kg, of which ui = 0.718 × 300 = 215.4 kJ/kg and flow work pivi = RTi = 0.287 × 300 = 86.1 kJ/kg.
   4. u₂ = cvT₂ = 301.5 kJ/kg, so T₂ = 301.5/0.718 = 419.9 K, which is γTi = 1.4 × 300 = 420 K within rounding: about 147 °C.
   5. Mass: m₂ = p₂V/(RT₂) = 500 × 0.2/(0.287 × 420) = 100/120.54 = 0.830 kg.
   6. Check: m₂u₂ = 0.8296 × 0.718 × 420 = 250.2 kJ and mihi = 0.8296 × 301.5 = 250.1 kJ; the balance closes within rounding.
   Answer: T₂ ≈ 420 K (147 °C) and m₂ = 0.830 kg. The 120 K rise comes from the flow work of 86.1 kJ/kg done by the line on the entering air. */
